  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The circle with equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=11$ is centered at $(${{field-1}}$,$ \n{{field-2}}$)$ and its radius is{{field-3}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The general equation of a circle with radius $r$ centered at the origin is \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, the equation ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=11$ represents a circle that is centered at the origin, with radius\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =11 \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we disregarded the negative solution for the radius since the radius of a circle must be positive.\n\nTherefore, the center is $(0,0)$ and the radius is $r=\\sqrt{11}.$",
